1. PURPOSE & CONTEXT                                                            

OWT is a software company with around 60 employees. When staffing a new project, admins currently have no efficient way to find the right people. They rely on memory or manually browsing profiles. This feature solves that: a single search lets the admin find employees whose skills and experience match the project’s requirements in seconds, not hours.   

2. OBJECTIVE 

Build a skills search feature that allows admins to find employees whose skills match a project's requirements. 

Both tiers below are in scope. They are implemented sequentially.  

    Tier 1 ships first as a standalone usable feature.  

    Tier 2 adds on top in a separate ticket.  

This proposal documents the full architecture for both tiers upfront so that Tier 1 is built with Tier 2 in mind (no rework needed when Tier 2 is added). 

 

Phase 1 - Tier 1: Keyword Search (implement first) 

    Fast structured SQL queries over skill/proficiency tables. 

    Deliverable: admin can search employees by skill, level, position. 

    Standalone - works without any embedding infrastructure. 

 

Phase 2 - Tier 2: Hybrid Semantic Search (implement after) 

Combines Tier 1 with vector similarity search over employee CVs, experience records, and training logs. 

    This is RAG minus the Generation step - retrieve and rank, not generate. 

    Deliverable: admin can also search by free text natural language query. 

    Depends on: pgvector extension + embedding service (Python FastAPI). 

 

3. TECHNOLOGY DECISIONS 

3.1 Vector Database: pgvector 

The project already uses PostgreSQL. pgvector is a PostgreSQL extension - no additional infrastructure, no extra service to deploy or maintain. 

Exact cosine similarity (no approximate index needed at 60 employees). 

Alternatives considered and ruled out: Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma, LanceDB, Elasticsearch, Typesense — all require a separate service. 

 

3.2 Embedding Model: all-MiniLM-L6-v2 

    384-dimension vectors 

    Runs on CPU (no GPU required) 

    Well-tested for semantic similarity tasks 

    Token limit: 256 tokens per chunk (input beyond this is silently truncated - chunking strategy in Section 4 is designed around this limit) 

    Can be swapped for a better model later without changing architecture 

 

3.3 Embedding Runtime: Python FastAPI service 

Since the project is migrating from NestJS to Python + FastAPI, the embedding service is built as part of the new Python backend - not a throwaway sidecar. NestJS calls it via HTTP during the transition period. 

After migration, everything is already in Python. Zero throwaway work. 

Library choice: onnxruntime over sentence_transformers: 

sentence_transformers pulls in PyTorch (~1.5 GB) as a dependency. 

For CPU-only inference on a small dataset this is disproportionate for a production internal app. Use onnxruntime + the ONNX export of all-MiniLM-L6-v2 instead (~50 MB) - lighter, faster cold start, no PyTorch in production. 

Trade-off: onnxruntime requires manually handling tokenization, padding, attention masks, and mean pooling (~20 lines vs 3 lines with sentence_transformers). If implementation complexity becomes a blocker, sentence_transformers is the fallback - the rest of the architecture is unaffected by this swap. 

 

from onnxruntime import InferenceSession 

from tokenizers import Tokenizer 

# Load model once at startup, reuse for all requests 

 

Security: 

The /embed endpoint is internal - called only by the NestJS/FastAPI app. It must be protected with a shared secret header (X-Internal-Token) and bound to localhost or an internal network interface only. This prevents any external process from embedding arbitrary text through the service. 

 

Example: 

@router.post("/embed") 

def embed(text: str, token: str = Header(..., alias="X-Internal-Token")): 

if token != settings.INTERNAL_TOKEN: 

raise HTTPException(status_code=401) 

return {"vector": model.encode(text).tolist()} 

 

 

 

 

