What gets indexed per employee - CHUNKING STRATEGY:  

all-MiniLM-L6-v2 has a hard 256-token limit. Text beyond that is silently truncated. Every source field is handled to stay within this limit. 

 

Source 
	

Field 
	

Chunking Strategy 

CV 
	

cv_overview.introduction 
	

Single chunk (short summary, fits within 256 tokens). The full cv.cv document is NOT embedded directly — it is too long and would be truncated. Use the introduction as the CV proxy. 

Experience 
	

roles_and_responsibilities 
	

One chunk per experience row. Truncate to 256 tokens if needed. (varchar 1024 ≈ ~200 tokens, safe.) 

Experience 
	

description 
	

One chunk per experience row. Combined with project name as prefix: "[ProjectName]: [description]" 

Training 
	

training_title + training_description 
	

One chunk per training row. Combined: "[Title]: [Description]". Truncate to 256 tokens if needed. 

Task (DONE only, assignees only) 
	

title + details 
	

One chunk per DONE task (assignees only, not createdBy). Combined: "[Title]: [Details]". Truncate to 256 tokens if needed. 

 

Index refresh: Real-time async re-index after every save. 

    At 60 employees, re-embedding one record takes ~10–50ms. 

    Re-index runs as a non-blocking background task after the HTTP response is returned to the client. Save feels instant; index updates within seconds. 

    Failure handling: fire-and-forget with error logging. If the embedding service is unavailable, the failure is logged and the stale index persists until the next successful save or a manual rebuild. This is acceptable for an internal tool at this scale. 

    Admin can trigger a re-index for a specific employee via PUT /api/v1/admin/skills-search/employees/:id to recover from stale index. 

    Hooks needed: CV save, experience save (create/update/delete), training save. 

 

Deactivated employees: When a user is set to is_active = false (standard offboarding path), their index rows are NOT deleted. Instead, all search queries filter by users.is_active = true at query time. This avoids unnecessary index churn and allows reactivation without re-indexing. 

Hard deletes (users.id removed) are handled by ON DELETE CASCADE. 

 

