How Tier 1 and Tier 2 combine: 

    Keyword only → Tier 1 SQL query only 

    Free text only → Tier 2 vector search only 

    Both → Keyword filters first → semantic reranks survivors final_score = (keyword_score × 0.6) + (semantic_score × 0.4) 

Score weights are defined as named constants in code:  

KEYWORD_WEIGHT = 0.6 

SEMANTIC_WEIGHT = 0.4 

These are the single place to change weighting. No config table needed at this scale. 

 

5.4 Free Text Query Processing (Tier 2) 

Step-by-step flow when admin enters free text: 

    Admin inputs: “React developer with e-commerce experience” 

    Preprocessing (before embedding): 

    Strip HTML/markdown tags if present 

    Normalize whitespace (collapse multiple spaces/newlines) 

    Truncate to 256 tokens if exceeded (hard limit of all-MiniLM-L6-v2) Note: do NOT lowercase or remove stop words — the transformer model handles these naturally and needs full context for best results. 

    Embed the query: POST /embed { “text”: “React developer with e-commerce experience” } Returns: vector[384] → [0.021, -0.143, 0.087, …] Same model (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) used for both indexing and querying — this is required for cosine similarity to be meaningful. 

    pgvector cosine similarity search: SELECT employee_id, chunk_text, metadata, 1 - (embedding <=> query_vector) AS score FROM skills_search_index JOIN users ON users.id = employee_id WHERE users.is_active = true ORDER BY score DESC 

    Aggregate by employee: Group results by employee_id. Take max score per employee (best matching chunk wins). 

    Combine with keyword score (if structured filters also provided): final_score = (keyword_score × 0.6) + (semantic_score × 0.4) 

    Return ranked employee list with matched chunk_text as semantic excerpt and metadata for display. 

